      "source_sha256": [
        "42416658bda0ffb660a2a92f6352889fbc23e67ef3b43d6929a4f08a8479ec2e"
      ],
      "title": "THE FROBENIUS GAIN ON THE DECOMPOSITION STEP IS A DIMENSION GAIN, NOT A SYMMETRY GAIN. On a subfield curve E/F_q attacked over F_{q^n}, the q-Frobenius is an EQUIVARIANCE between the n conjugate descended Semaev ideals I_R, I_{R^pi}, ..., never a symmetry of one of them, and it acts freely on the union of their solutions; so an invariant-ring rewriting of the orbit system has the plain system's Bezout count and needs generators of degree n, and no solving-degree drop is predicted (arm A, a closure with a named obstruction). The Frobenius object that can move an exponent is the trace-zero variety T_n = ker(1 + pi + ... + pi^(n-1)), an abelian variety of dimension n - 1 over F_q that contains the prime-order subgroup G; a factor base cut out inside T_n decomposes with n - 1 summands, and Gaudry's abelian-variety exponent reads q^(2 - 2/(n-1)) on the object against q^(2 - 2/n) on the matched non-subfield null at the same N (arm B, predicted exponent difference 2/(n(n-1)) in q, toy-testable as a measured ratio at n = 3 and n = 5)",
      "source_statuses": [
        "proposed"
      ],
      "recommended_priority": "high",
      "areas": [
        "FROB"
      ],
      "ecc": true,
      "classification_unresolved": false,
